WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:05.049
Avengers: Endgame was heavy on the fan service
cameos, but one brief appearance was meant

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:06.240
for a specific type of fan.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.950
During one key scene in the multi-billion-dollar
blockbuster, sharp-eyed viewers spotted Matthew

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.610
Berry, the ESPN fantasy sports expert, as
a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:20.000
agent an appearance made possible by co-director
Joe Russo's fantasy football obsession.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.540
Berry was easy to miss, due to the fact that
his turn was slightly overshadowed by another,

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.300
much more high-profile cameo.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:32.340
It came during the scene in which Tony Stark,
Steve Rogers, and Scott Lang, having just

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.070
snagged the Tesseract during their return
trip to 2012's Battle of New York, are confronted

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:40.260
by a phalanx of agents insisting that they
turn it over to S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:40.760
custody.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.820
They are led by Alexander Pierce, whom the
pair know — thanks to the events of Captain

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.240
America: The Winter Soldier — to be a HYDRA
turncoat.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:53.840
Mayhem ensues, and Loki ends up escaping with
the Tesseract — but not before Berry was

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.060
given his brief time to shine as one of the
agents flanking Pierce.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:02.660
It's not often that a fantasy sports guru,
even an extremely successful and knowledgeable

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.080
one, finds himself sharing the frame with
the biggest stars in the Marvel Cinematic

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.880
Universe and one stone-cold screen icon in
one of the biggest movies of all time.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.880
But according to Berry, it all came about
due to Russo's familiarity with his work,

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.120
owing to the director's rabid fantasy fandom.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.200
Speaking in an interview with io9, he said,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.120
"It happened through the grace of Joe Russo.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.800
He's been reading my column for years, and
he's a big fantasy football player.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.560
And about two years ago he gave me a call
and said 'Hey man, I got a part that I think

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.160
is perfect for you if you want to be in the
movie'.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.160
And I was like 'Yeah, of course,' obviously."

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.619
Berry went on to explain that he was told
that he'd be playing somebody, quote, "official,"

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.340
and that he'd be sharing the screen with Thor,
the Hulk, and Iron Man.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.900
His head was still presumably spinning in
disbelief when he got a call from someone

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:52.340
in Endgame's costume department to get his
suit size, which led him to believe that his

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.360
part would that of a police detective or the
like — since, of course, S.H.I.E.L.D. had

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.800
long since disbanded, and the flick's time
travel elements weren't yet public knowledge.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.780
"Somebody peed my pants."

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:03.780
"Time travel!"

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.239
When he arrived on set in November 2017, Berry
signed a phone book's worth of NDAs before

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:13.819
getting his hands on his script pages which
simply confused the heck out of him.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:14.819
According to Berry,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.120
"They hand me the script, it's like two pages…
and it makes no sense to me.

00:02:18.549 --> 00:02:19.820
All it is is the dialogue.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.200
I see dialogue between Alexander Pierce, my
character, Tony Stark, and Thor.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.160
Then, you see like one or two lines from 'Other
Tony' and I'm [saying], 'What the heck is

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:29.740
Other Tony?'"

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:31.880
"You had to ask."

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.800
Once the actual business of shooting the scene
commenced, Berry copped to being just as star-struck

00:02:36.809 --> 00:02:39.019
as the rest of us would have been in his situation,
saying,

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:39.740
"Forget actors.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:41.440
Forget movie stars.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.519
I'm surrounded by iconic legends.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.820
I mean, I'm in a frame with Robert Downey,
Jr., Robert Redford, Chris Hemsworth, and

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:48.320
me.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.640
Like, one of the shots in the movie is literally
just [the] four of us.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.140
That's bananas to think about."

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.980
Of course, as soon as it dawned on him what
the scene's setting was and who he was sharing

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
it with, Berry became keenly aware of just
why he'd had to sign all those NDAs.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.260
Discussing how he put the pieces together,
Berry recalled,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.020
"I'm in the lobby of Stark Tower.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.340
They're wearing what they were wearing in
The Avengers.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.000
Clearly, it's either a flashback or it's time
travel.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.420
Also, Alexander Pierce is there.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.160
He's dead."

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.340
Of course, for roughly a year and a half until
Endgame's release, he couldn't share a single

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:23.269
detail of the scene that he'd shot including
the fact that he'd literally been in frame

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:26.300
with Robert freaking Redford with anybody,
not even his wife.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.139
As if hobnobbing with one of the greatest
actors of all time weren't cool enough, Berry

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.820
went on to relate that Tony Stark himself
had gone out of his way to encourage him during

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:37.339
the shoot, saying,

00:03:37.339 --> 00:03:38.590
"Downey turned to me and said 'You're doing
very well.'

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.609
He said, 'Don't take this the wrong way, but
you look like a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:42.320
agent.'

00:03:42.609 --> 00:03:44.860
And I said, 'Robert, coming from you that's
an amazing compliment.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:45.740
I will take that.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.200
Thank you very much."

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:50.920
"Kid, you're an Avenger now."

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.120
Berry wrapped up the interview by noting that
he felt he'd nailed his role by virtue of,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.939
well, not standing out at all.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:03:59.460
As he put it,

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.979
"The scene is about Tony Stark and Thor running
into Alexander Pierce while they have Loki

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:04.600
and the Tesseract.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.380
That's what the scene is about.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.120
You don't want anyone to take you out of that
moment.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.779
The fact that they just go 'Oh, look.

00:04:10.779 --> 00:04:11.779
There's a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.840
guy there' makes me feel like I did a good
job."

00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:19.220
We concur, and we'll be anxiously awaiting
the announcement of the standalone flick What's-His-Name:

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.230
Agent of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.570
(Or Maybe HYDRA) for Marvel's Phase 4.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.140
His greatest strength?

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.300
Fantasy picks you can count on at least, until
the entire organization up and betrays you.

